+
+# Scheduled posts are hidden by a read-time filter
+
+## Context
+
+Authors want to write a post now and have it go public later. Until then
+the post must exist only for its author, and at the chosen time it must
+become public with nobody pressing a button and nothing running at that
+moment.
+
+## Decision
+
+`posts.published_at` is a nullable text column holding an ISO 8601 UTC
+timestamp. `NULL` or a past instant means public; a future instant means
+scheduled. There is no status column and no job: a post becomes public
+because the clock passes its date.
+
+- **One filter.** `Post.visibleTo(viewerId)` is the only place the rule
+  lives: public posts, plus the viewer's own scheduled ones. Every read that
+  serves posts to a viewer starts from it. A new read of posts must too.
+- **Text comparison.** The filter compares `published_at` as text, which
+  orders chronologically only while every value has the same form. The
+  controller therefore stores `new Date(publishAt).toISOString()`, whatever
+  offset the request used. Anything that writes the column directly must
+  write that form.
+- **The request field is `publishAt`**, on `POST /posts` and
+  `PUT /posts/:id`. It needs a zone (`Z` or an offset): the server cannot
+  know which zone a bare local time was typed in, so the form converts the
+  browser's local time before sending.
+- **Absent means publish now, on update as well.** `PUT` replaces the date
+  like every other field. The edit form always sends the loaded date back;
+  a client that leaves `publishAt` out of an update publishes the post.
+- **404 before 403.** Edit, update and delete look the post up through the
+  same filter before asking the policy, so someone else's scheduled post
+  answers 404. A 403 would confirm that it exists. Public posts keep
+  answering 403.
+- **The home page shows public posts only**, to the author as well. It has
+  no "scheduled" marker, so a scheduled post listed there would read as
+  live.
+- **`posts:scheduled` owns stdout.** `bin/console.ts` sends `console.info`
+  and `console.log` to stderr while the app boots, because the ORM reports
+  the migrations it applies on stdout and the report is piped.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Publishing needs no scheduler, queue or cron, and cannot be missed by one
+that is down. The price is that visibility is a query concern: a read that
+skips `Post.visibleTo()` leaks scheduled posts, and nothing in the type
+system stops it. Nothing happens at the moment a post goes live, so a
+notification or a cache purge at that moment would need a scheduler after
+all.
